{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartBBranchPointSum","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartBBranchPointSum","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartAConvergence"],"declarations":13,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.wp1_riemann_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.wp1_riemann_bound","kind":"theorem","line":298,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L298-L325","doc":"**V3 assembly — the W-P1 Riemann bound.**  For `1 < q` and `x·qⁿ < 1`, the\npositive-factor rescaled log-sum `(1−q)·Σ_{k<n} log(1−xqᵏ)` approximates the\ndilog difference `Li₂(xqⁿ) − Li₂(x)` from below, with error bounded by the\nexplicit boundary decomposition:\n\n`0 ≤ (Li₂(xqⁿ) − Li₂(x)) − (1−q)Σ log(1−xqᵏ)`\n`  ≤ (q−1)·(−log(1−xqⁿ)) − (q−1)·qⁿ·(−log(1−x))`.\n\nAssembles the telescoping bound (`riemann_error_bound_up`), the change of\nvariables (`integral_one_to_c_eq_LiTwo_sub`), the log-sum identity\n(`partialSum_up_eq`) and the error decomposition (`error_up_eq`).  As\n`q → 1⁺` with `xqⁿ → 1⁻`, `Li₂(xqⁿ) → π²/6` (brick 2's Abel limit), so W-P1\nconverges to `Li₂(x) − π²/6` **iff** the boundary term `(q−1)·log(1−xqⁿ) → 0`\n— the single remaining scalar limit (controlled by the Faddeev scaling). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.error_up_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.error_up_eq","kind":"theorem","line":285,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L285-L296","doc":"**V3 brick 3f — the error decomposition.**  The `riemann_error_bound_up`\nmajorant factors exactly as a boundary log-term minus a manifestly-vanishing\nterm:\n`qⁿ(q−1)(h(qⁿ)−h(1)) = (q−1)·(−log(1−xqⁿ)) − (q−1)·qⁿ·(−log(1−x))`,\nusing `qⁿ·h(qⁿ) = −log(1−xqⁿ)`.  The second term `→ 0` (since `qⁿ ≤ 1/x` is\nbounded and `q−1 → 0`), so the whole W-P1 error reduces to the single\nboundary limit `(q−1)·log(1−xqⁿ) → 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.integral_one_to_c_eq_LiTwo_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.integral_one_to_c_eq_LiTwo_sub","kind":"theorem","line":261,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L261-L283","doc":"**V3 brick 3e — the going-up integral evaluates to a `Li₂` difference.**\nFor `0 < x`, `1 ≤ c`, `x·c < 1`: `∫_1^c −log(1−xu)/u du = Li₂(xc) − Li₂(x)`,\nvia the change of variables `t = x u` (`smul_integral_comp_mul_left`) plus\nbrick 1 (`integral_Icc_eq_LiTwo_sub`).  This turns the Riemann-sum target\n`∫_1^{qⁿ} h` of `riemann_error_bound_up` into `Li₂(xqⁿ) − Li₂(x)`, whose\nlimit as `xqⁿ → 1⁻` is `π²/6 − Li₂(x)` (brick 2's Abel limit). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.partialSum_up_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.partialSum_up_eq","kind":"theorem","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L245-L259","doc":"**V3 brick 3d — the Riemann sum IS the rescaled log-sum.**  Term-by-term,\n`(−log(1−xqᵏ)/qᵏ)·(qᵏ⁺¹−qᵏ) = (1−q)·log(1−xqᵏ)`, since `(qᵏ⁺¹−qᵏ)/qᵏ = q−1`.\nSo the going-up Riemann sum equals `(1−q)·Σ_{k<n} log(1−xqᵏ)`, i.e. `−` the\npositive-factor W-P1 quantity `−(1−q)·Σ log(1−xqᵏ)`.  This connects\n`riemann_error_bound_up` to the log-product without needing the geometric\n`riemann_sum_form`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.riemann_error_bound_up","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.riemann_error_bound_up","kind":"theorem","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L181-L243","doc":"**V3 brick 3c — going-up Riemann-sum error bound.**  For `1 < q` and\n`x·qⁿ < 1` (all cells below the branch point):\n`0 ≤ ∫_1^{qⁿ} h − Σ_{k<n} h(qᵏ)·(qᵏ⁺¹−qᵏ) ≤ qⁿ·(q−1)·(h(qⁿ)−h(1))`,\nwith `h(u) = −log(1−xu)/u`.  Telescoping induction on the per-cell squeeze\n(brick 3b), the going-up analogue of Part A's `riemann_error_bound`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.h_integral_ge_left_Icc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.h_integral_ge_left_Icc","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L156-L169","doc":"**Lower cell bound.**  `h(a)·(b−a) ≤ ∫_a^b h` on `[a,b] ⊆ (0, 1/x)`\n(`h` increasing ⇒ `h(a)` is the min). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.h_integral_le_right_Icc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.h_integral_le_right_Icc","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L141-L154","doc":"**Upper cell bound.**  `∫_a^b h ≤ h(b)·(b−a)` on `[a,b] ⊆ (0, 1/x)`\n(`h` increasing ⇒ `h(b)` is the max). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.h_intervalIntegrable_Icc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.h_intervalIntegrable_Icc","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L133-L139","doc":"`h` is interval-integrable on any `[a,b] ⊆ (0, 1/x)` (`x·b < 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.h_continuousOn_Icc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.h_continuousOn_Icc","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L123-L131","doc":"Continuity of `h(u) = −log(1 − x u)/u` on `[a,b] ⊆ (0, 1/x)` (`x·b < 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.h_monotoneOn_Ioc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.h_monotoneOn_Ioc","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L101-L113","doc":"Monotonicity of `h(u) = −log(1 − x u)/u` on `(0, b]` whenever `x·b < 1` — the\ngoing-up generalisation of `h_monotoneOn` (which is `b = 1`).  Convex-slope:\n`h(u) = (g u − g 0)/(u − 0)` is a secant slope from `0` of the convex `g`,\nhence monotone (`ConvexOn.secant_mono`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.convexOn_neg_log_one_sub_mul_Icc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.convexOn_neg_log_one_sub_mul_Icc","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L82-L99","doc":"Convexity of `g(u) = −log(1 − x u)` on `[0, b]` whenever `x·b < 1` — the\ngoing-up generalisation of `convexOn_neg_log_one_sub_mul` (which is `b = 1`).\n`log` is concave on `(0,∞)`, `u ↦ 1 − x u` is affine mapping `[0,b]` into\n`(0,∞)` (since `x u ≤ x b < 1`), negation flips concave to convex. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.tendsto_integral_x_to_c_nhdsWithin_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.tendsto_integral_x_to_c_nhdsWithin_one","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L50-L72","doc":"**V3 brick 2 — the improper target integral (branch-point limit).**  For\n`0 < x < 1`, as the upper endpoint `c → 1⁻` the interior integral converges to\nthe branch-point value:\n\n`∫_x^c −log(1−t)/t  ⟶  π²/6 − Li₂(x)`.\n\nCombines brick 1 (`= Li₂(c) − Li₂(x)` on `[x,1)`) with the corpus Abel limit\n`tendsto_LiTwo_nhdsWithin_lt_one` (`Li₂(c) → π²/6`).  Since\n`∫_x^1 −log(1−t)/t = Li₂(1) − Li₂(x) = π²/6 − Li₂(x)`, this is the target the\npositive-factor Riemann sum (`W-P1`) converges to — the last, singular, cell\nof the `q > 1` geometric partition contributing the cusp regulator `ζ(2)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.integral_Icc_eq_LiTwo_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.integral_Icc_eq_LiTwo_sub","kind":"theorem","line":28,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointSum.lean#L28-L48","doc":"**V3 brick 1 — the interior target integral.**  For `0 < x ≤ c < 1`,\n`∫_x^c −log(1−t)/t = Li₂(c) − Li₂(x)`.  Since `−log(1−t)/t` is the derivative\nof `Li₂` (`hasDerivAt_LiTwo`) and both endpoints lie in `(0,1)`, this is a\nproper FTC — the integrand is continuous on `[x,c]` (no `t=0` or branch-point\nsingularity).  Taking `c → 1⁻` (with `LiTwo_one`) will give the branch-point\nvalue `π²/6 − Li₂(x)` (V3 brick 2, the improper step). ","signatureChunk":0}]}